[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Filtering Utilities in TypeScript: Pick, Omit, Extract, and Exclude"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript provides powerful built-in utility types that allow developers to transform existing types dynamically. Among the most commonly used transformations are filtering utilities. These utilities help you select, remove, or isolate specific properties or types without rewriting code."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. The Pick Utility"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Pick<T, K> utility constructs a type by selecting a set of properties K from an existing type T. This is ideal when you need a subset of a larger interface or model."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  isAdmin: boolean;
}

type UserProfile = Pick<User, 'name' | 'email'>;

const profile: UserProfile = {
  name: 'Jane Doe',
  email: 'jane@example.com'
};"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. The Omit Utility"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Conversely, the Omit<T, K> utility constructs a type by picking all properties from T and then removing K. This is extremely useful when dealing with payloads where certain fields like IDs or timestamps are generated automatically by the server."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"interface Product {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  price: number;
  createdAt: Date;
}

type CreateProductDTO = Omit<Product, 'id' | 'createdAt'>;

const newProduct: CreateProductDTO = {
  title: 'Mechanical Keyboard',
  price: 99.99
};"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"3. The Extract Utility"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Pick and Omit operate on object properties, Extract<T, U> works on union types. It constructs a type by extracting all union members from T that are assignable to U."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"type AllPermissions = 'read' | 'write' | 'execute' | 'admin';
type BasicPermissions = Extract<AllPermissions, 'read' | 'write'>;

// BasicPermissions results in 'read' | 'write'"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"4. The Exclude Utility"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Exclude<T, U> utility is the exact opposite of Extract. It constructs a type by excluding all union members from T that are assignable to U."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"type ResponseStatus = 'Success' | 'Error' | 'Loading' | 'Unauthorized';
type ActiveStatus = Exclude<ResponseStatus, 'Loading'>;

// ActiveStatus results in 'Success' | 'Error' | 'Unauthorized'"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Summary of Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Pick when you want to explicitly white-list properties from an object type."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Omit when you want to black-list specific properties, such as system-managed fields."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Extract to filter union types based on intersection criteria."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Exclude to filter out unwanted types or string literals from an existing union."}]
